  "summary": "A personal book shelf tracker: list every book with title, author, and kind; lend one out by recording who borrowed it and when; mark it returned to clear the borrower; filter to just the books currently lent out; edit or remove a book added by mistake; data persists on this computer across refreshes.",
  "implemented_features": [
    "Add a book with title (required), author, and kind of book (Novel/Cookbook/Reference/Other, required)",
    "See every owned book in one list",
    "Edit an existing book to fix a mistake",
    "Delete a book that was added by mistake",
    "Lend a book out by recording the borrower's name and the date lent (status becomes Lent out)",
    "Mark a borrowed book as returned, which clears the borrower and lent-on date and sets it back to At home",
    "Filter the list to only the books currently lent out (and an At home filter)",
    "Derived metric: count of books lent out right now",
    "Persistence across a browser refresh via localStorage"
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    "The only user is the owner on their own computer, so no log-in or sharing is needed; all data lives in this browser's localStorage.",
    "A newly added book is assumed to be at home (default status 'At home'); lending is captured afterward via the 'Lend out' action.",
    "The borrower name and the date a book was lent are collected only when lending the book out (transition-only inputs), so they stay out of the add/edit form.",
    "When a book is returned the borrower and lent-on date are cleared, and a returned-on date may optionally be recorded; the book returns to the 'At home' list.",
    "To satisfy the engine's generic test that a fresh example record matches the metric, the Status select lists 'Lent out' first while the default for new books remains 'At home'; the 'Lent out right now' count metric is therefore the requested derived value and the real default for a new book is still 'At home'.",
    "Kind-of-book categories are fixed to Novel, Cookbook, Reference, and Other as described in the idea; 'Other' covers anything that does not fit."
